---
title: Concepts & Architecture
description: Runtime lifecycle, frame order, and renderer rebuild boundaries.
---

Spektral separates immutable pipeline declarations from mutable frame data. This boundary controls caching, renderer recreation, and the order in which work reaches the GPU.

## Declaration and runtime layers

| Layer | Owns | Changes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Material | Fragment source, uniforms and their types, texture definitions, defines, includes, storage-buffer definitions | Create a new material |
| Canvas | Device options, color pipeline, passes and their immutable resource topology, render targets, render mode, DPR | Update `FragCanvas` props |
| Frame | Uniform values, texture sources, storage-buffer writes, invalidation | Use `FrameState` or runtime context methods |

`defineMaterial(...)` validates and freezes its result. `resolveMaterial(...)` preprocesses WGSL, builds the uniform layout, resolves texture and storage metadata, and computes a deterministic material signature.

## Initialization

When `FragCanvas` mounts, its adapter:

1. creates the frame registry and runtime contexts;
2. starts the framework-neutral runtime loop;
3. resolves the material;
4. requests the WebGPU adapter and device;
5. creates buffers, textures, bind groups, and render or compute pipelines as needed.

Before the first frame, the renderer asynchronously prepares every active built-in fullscreen
pipeline for its resolved input and output formats. Shader hot edits prepare in the background and
keep the last-known-good pipeline until the latest source succeeds.

If initialization fails, the runtime reports the normalized error and retries with exponential backoff from 250 ms to an 8-second cap. A pipeline-signature change resets the backoff.

## Frame order

Each animation-frame tick follows this order:

1. Update time, clamped delta, and canvas size.
2. Run scheduled `useFrame` tasks in resolved stage and task order.
3. Evaluate `autoRender` and the active render mode.
4. When rendering, merge material defaults with runtime overrides, flush pending storage-buffer writes, upload eligible textures, and write dirty uniform ranges.
5. Resolve external compute providers, validate resource accesses, and execute each compute graph segment in stable dependency order. Fragment-feedback passes remain opaque barriers in their declaration positions.
6. Render the base material.
7. Execute enabled render passes through the slot graph.
8. Present the final surface, applying the private color presentation pass when configured.
9. Clear one-frame invalidation and manual-advance state.

Compute and fragment-feedback passes are always pre-scene. Their position relative to render passes in the `passes` array cannot place them between post-processing passes.

## Compute resource graph

Each compute descriptor resolves to a logical resource, a physical GPU allocation, and a read or write role. The same resolved model drives shader bindings and scheduling.

For every consecutive compute segment, the planner:

1. finds the single writer for each logical resource;
2. places `version: 'current'` readers after that writer;
3. places `version: 'initial'` readers before it;
4. adds publication edges from ping-pong writers to downstream readers;
5. performs a stable topological sort, using declaration order only for independent passes.

Multiple writers, dependency cycles, and overlapping sampled/write subresources fail before command submission. A fragment-feedback pass splits compute segments because it has no compute resource descriptors. Work never moves across that barrier.

Material textures and buffers enter a frame as the `initial` version. A normal writer or completed ping-pong batch publishes `current`. The base fragment render reads the latest published view. Ping-pong A/B allocations and parity are renderer internals.

## Renderer rebuild boundary

| Change | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Preprocessed fragment source or define value | Rebuild |
| Uniform name or type | Rebuild |
| Texture definition, including sampling, upload, storage, dimensions, format, or `fragmentVisible` | Rebuild |
| Storage-buffer name, size, type, access, or `initialData` | Rebuild |
| Pass resource topology or external-resource metadata | New pass instance; compute pipeline/layout selected for the new topology |
| `color`, `adapterOptions`, or `deviceDescriptor` | Rebuild |
| Runtime uniform value | Dirty-range buffer update |
| Runtime texture source | Upload; reallocate only when required by size or format |
| Runtime storage-buffer write | Flush before rendering or compute dispatch |
| Canvas resize | Resize dependent surfaces; no renderer recreation |
| `clearColor`, render mode, `autoRender`, `maxDelta`, or DPR | Apply through runtime state |

Material signatures cover material-owned declarations. The runtime combines them with canvas-owned pipeline inputs to decide whether to destroy and recreate the renderer.

Physical resource identity is not part of a compute pipeline key. A per-frame video upload, resized owned texture, or external provider swap can refresh a bind group while reusing the same pipeline. External providers are evaluated once per pass per rendered frame so graph validation and command encoding see the same object.

## Render graph

The base scene and post-processing graph use these slots:

| Slot | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `source` | Current scene or pass result |
| `target` | Ping-pong companion for swapping passes |
| `canvas` | Logical final output; output-only |
| Render-target name | Declared off-screen surface |

Without post-process passes or a final color pipeline, the base material renders directly to the canvas. A color pipeline can make `canvas` a logical internal target; Spektral then presents it to the physical swapchain after all render passes.

The planner rejects invalid swaps, reads from `canvas`, unknown named targets, and reads before the target has been written in the current frame. [Render Passes](/docs/render-passes) documents slot routing.

## Teardown

On unmount, the adapter stops the animation loop, destroys renderer-owned GPU resources and its owned device, clears the scheduler, and removes adapter lifecycle registrations.
